\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s10 \li1209 \fi-1007 \ri1007 \ql Class Description\
\fs14\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 NSColorPanel provides a standard user interface for selecting color in an application. It provides a number of standard color selection modes, and, with the NSColorPickingDefault and NSColorPickingCustom protocols, allows an application to add its own color selection modes. It allows the user to save swatches containing frequently used colors. Once set, these swatches are displayed by NSColorPanel in any application where it is used, giving the user color consistency between applications. NSColorPanel enables users to capture a color anywhere on the screen for use in the active application, and allows dragging colors from itself into views in an application. NSColorPanel\'27s action message is sent to the target object when the user changes the current color. \
\fs14\
\fs28 An application has only one instance of NSColorPanel, the shared instance. Invoking the \b sharedColorPanel: \b0 method returns the shared instance of NSColorPanel, instantiating it if necessary. \
\fs14\
\fs28 You can put NSColorPanel in any application created with Interface Builder by adding the \'aaColors...\'ba item from the Menu palette to the application\'27s menu. \
\fs14\
\fs26\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s58 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql Color Mask and Color Modes\
\fs8\
\fs6\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 The color mask determines which of the color modes are enabled for NSColorPanel. This mask is set before you initialize a new instance of NSColorPanel. NSColorPanelAllModesMask represents the logical OR of the other color mask constants: It causes the NSColorPanel to display all standard color pickers. When initializing a new instance of NSColorPanel, you can logically OR any combination of color mask constants to restrict the available color modes. \
\fs14\
\pard \s63 \li1915 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx5745 \tx6498 \tx7257 \tx9547 \tx10281 \tx11037 \f0 \b \fs28 Mode\tab Color Mask Constant\
\fs6\
\pard \s61 \li1915 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx5745 \tx6497 \tx7257 \tx9547 \tx10281 \tx11037 \f1 \b0 \fs28 Grayscale-Alpha\tab NSColorPanelGrayModeMask\
\fi0 Red-Green-Blue\tab NSColorPanelRGBModeMask \
\fi0 Cyan-Yellow-Magenta-Black\tab NSColorPanelCMYKModeMask\
\fi0 Hue-Saturation-Brightness\tab NSColorPanelHSBModeMask\
\fi0 Custom palette\tab NSColorPanelCustomPaletteModeMask\
\fi0 Custom color list\tab NSColorPanelColorListModeMask \
\fi0 Color wheel\tab NSColorPanelWheelModeMask \
\fi0 All of the above\tab NSColorPanelAllModesMask \
\fs14\
\pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 \fs28 The NSColorPanel\'27s color mode mask is set using the class method \b setPickerMask:\b0 . The mask must be set before creating an application\'27s instance of NSColorPanel. \
\fs14\
\fs28 When an application\'27s instance of NSColorPanel is masked for more than one color mode, your program can set its active mode by invoking the \b setMode:\b0  method with a color mode constant as its argument; the user can set the mode by clicking buttons on the panel. Here are the standard color modes and mode constants:\
\fs14\
\pard \s63 \li1915 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx5745 \tx6498 \tx7257 \tx9547 \tx10281 \tx11037 \f0 \b \fs28 Mode\tab Color Mode Constant\
\fs6\
\pard \s61 \li1915 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx5745 \tx6497 \tx7257 \tx9547 \tx10281 \tx11037 \f1 \b0 \fs28 Grayscale-Alpha\tab NSGrayModeColorPanel\
\fi0 Red-Green-Blue\tab NSRGBModeColorPanel \
\fi0 Cyan-Yellow-Magenta-Black\tab NSCMYKModeColorPanel\
\fi0 Hue-Saturation-Brightness\tab NSHSBModeColorPanel\
\fi0 Custom palette\tab NSCustomPaletteModeColorPanel \
\fi0 Custom color list\tab NSColorListModeColorPanel \
\fi0 Color wheel\tab NSWheelModeColorPanel \
\fs14\
\pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 \fs28 In grayscale-alpha, red-green-blue, cyan-magenta-yellow-black, and hue-saturation-brightness modes, the user adjusts colors by manipulating sliders. In the custom palette mode, the user can load an NSImage file (TIFF or EPS) into the NSColorPanel, then select colors from the image. In custom color list mode, the user can create and load lists of named colors. The two custom modes provide NSPopUpButtons for loading and saving files. Finally, color wheel mode provides a simplified control for selecting colors. \
\fs14\
\fs28 If a color panel has been used, it uses whatever mode it was in last as the default mode when NSColorPanelAllModesMask is used to initialize the NSColorPanel. Otherwise, it uses color wheel mode.\
\fs14\
\fs26\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s58 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql Associated Classes and Protocols\
\fs8\
\fs6\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 The NSColorList class provides an API for managing custom color lists. The NSColorPanel methods \b attachColorList:\b0  and \b detachColorList:\b0  let your application add and remove custom lists from the NSColorPanel\'27s user interface. \
\fs14\
\fs28 The protocols NSColorPickingDefault and NSColorPickingCustom provide an API for adding custom color selection to the user interface. The NSColorPicker class implements the NSColorPickingDefault protocol; you can subclass NSColorPicker and implement the NSColorPickingCustom protocol in your subclass to create your own user interface for color selection. \
\fs14\
\pard \s57 \li2804 \fi-1292 \ri1007 \ql \tx2799 \tx3282 \tx3771 \fs28 \f0 \b \fs24 See also:\tab \f1 \b0 \fs28 NSColorList, NSColorPickingDefault, NSColorPicker, NSColorPickingDefault protocol, NSColorPickingCustom protocol, NSColorWell\
\fs14\
